@section('description')New and edit pages draw a form from the fields of a resource, with text, number, date and checkbox inputs. Help text and file fields are not built yet.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>The new and edit pages draw a form from the resource's <code>fields</code>. When the form is submitted, the resource's <code>validate</code> method, or <code>validate_update</code> on an edit, turns the values into its input, or returns a message for each field.</p>

  <h2>Field kinds</h2>
  <p>Each field has a kind, which sets the input and how the value is read:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Text</code>, a single-line text input.</li>
    <li><code>Textarea</code>, a multi-line text input.</li>
    <li><code>Password</code>, a password input. The form never fills it in, not even on edit.</li>
    <li><code>Number</code>, a number input. The value is read as text, and the resource parses it.</li>
    <li><code>Checkbox</code>, a tick box. A tick sends <code>on</code>, and an empty box sends nothing, so the value is false.</li>
    <li><code>Decimal</code>, a decimal number input, with the browser's step. The value is read as a number, such as <code>9.5</code>.</li>
    <li><code>Select</code>, a list of <code>(value, label)</code> choices.</li>
    <li><code>Lookup</code>, a select whose choices come from the database each time the form is shown. A derived resource fills it with <code>options = ...</code> or <code>belongs_to = ...</code>.</li>
    <li><code>Date</code>, a date input. The value is read as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.</li>
    <li><code>DateTime</code>, a date and time input. The value is read as <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM</code>, with seconds when given.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A hand-written resource can use every kind. The derive writes the kinds from the field's type and attributes, as <a href="/docs/resources">Resources</a> shows. A derived password field is a <code>String</code>, and it is hashed on save. See <a href="/docs/resources#passwords">Resources</a>.</p>
  <p>A select starts with a <code>Select an option</code> placeholder, so a form that is submitted without a choice does not send the first one by accident. The derive checks a submitted <code>Select</code> value against its listed choices. The panel checks a submitted <code>Lookup</code> value against the resource's <code>options</code> method, and refuses one that is not listed with <code>Choose one of the options.</code>. A hand-written <code>Select</code> is checked by its <code>validate</code>, which must do that itself.</p>

  <h2>Required fields</h2>
  <p>A field marked <code>required</code> shows an asterisk after its label. The asterisk is only a hint: the resource's <code>validate</code> must reject a blank value. The derive does this for you, and a hand-written resource calls <code>check_required</code>, which trims the value and records the message <code>&lt;label&gt; is required.</code> if it is blank.</p>
  <p>A value made only of spaces counts as blank, and so does a required field the form did not send. A derived field that is not an <code>Option</code> is required on create and on edit: a blank one is refused, the form shows again with the message, and nothing is saved. A checkbox is never blank, because an empty box is false.</p>

  <h2 id="clearing">Clearing an optional field on edit</h2>
  <p><strong>Ignite 0.3.0: not yet at the pinned revision.</strong> This section describes Ignite 0.3.0, which is not published yet. The revision <code>d3d1975</code> that <a href="/docs/install">Installation</a> pins does not have it. At that revision, a derived resource skips a blank optional field on edit and keeps the old value.</p>
  <p>A field that is not required may be left blank. On create, a blank optional field is saved as NULL. On edit, it is saved as NULL too, so emptying a field and saving clears the stored value, as WordPress clears a field you empty and update. This is how a derived resource and a runtime resource save, since Ignite 0.3.0. Before 0.3.0, a derived resource skipped a blank optional field on edit and kept the old value.</p>
  <p>The panel's forms send every field. A request that does not send an optional field at all, such as a partial form an app posts itself, leaves that field as it is on edit. A checkbox that is not sent is unticked, so false.</p>
  <p>A password is the exception: a blank password on edit keeps the stored hash. See <a href="#passwords">Passwords</a>. A hand-written resource decides what its own <code>update</code> writes. See <a href="/docs/resources#saving">Resources</a>.</p>

  <h2>Validation errors</h2>
  <p>When <code>validate</code> returns errors, a <code>FieldErrors</code> map from each field name (a <code>String</code>) to its message, the panel shows the form again with the values the user typed, a message under each field that has one, and the status 422. Nothing is written to the database. A derived field that cannot be read, such as a number that is not a whole number, gets its own message, such as <code>Price must be a whole number.</code></p>
  <p>A value that clashes with a unique index is also a field error. A second user with the same email, for example, shows <code>Email is already taken.</code> under the email field, with the status 422. Any other database error is a 500.</p>

  <h2 id="passwords">Passwords</h2>
  <p>A password field is never filled in on the edit page. On create, a password is required. On edit, a blank password keeps the stored hash, and a new one is hashed with argon2 on save. A hand-written resource hashes with <code>ignite_core::hash_password</code> itself, and overrides <code>validate_update</code> to allow a blank password on edit. See <a href="/docs/resources#passwords">Resources</a>.</p>

  <h2>Forms and tokens</h2>
  <p>Every panel form carries a hidden <code>_token</code> field, which the panel checks on submit. An app's own forms do the same, with <code>CsrfForm</code>. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a>. A form that was open too long shows the Page Expired page, with status 419, and nothing is saved. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a>.</p>

  <h2>Not built yet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Help text and file fields.</strong> A field has a label and a kind. There is no help text, and no upload.</li>
  </ul>
@endsection
